{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>My doctrine shall drop as the rain.</b> That is, according to the saying of the Sages of blessed memory (Avot 3:17) ״אִם אֵין קֶמַח אֵין תּוֹרָה״ [If there is no flour there is no Torah] etc., the verse intends in this statement that just as HaShem gives rain to sustain and provide, so too they need to study Torah. And this is the measure of the verse: let it drip just as I drip the rain for my doctrine — as if it said ״יַעֲרֹף לִקְחִי כַּמָּטָר״ [let my doctrine drip as the rain] — for the Holy One, blessed be He, does not demand of a person to study Torah until He prepares for him the means of his livelihood. And from the conclusion of the matter you learn that if they do not drip drops of Torah from their mouths, HaShem has no desire to give rain. And this is what we explained in the saying of the Tanna (Avot 3:17) ״אִם אֵין קֶמַח אֵין תּוֹרָה״ — meaning, if you see that there is no flour, know that there is no Torah. And the reason he described Torah study with this expression ״יַעֲרֹף״ [shall drop] is to liken it to the act of rain, which drips." "<b>Further,</b> it hints that just as withholding rain surrounds a person with death, so too withholding Torah will be his undoing, in the manner of what it says (Isaiah 1:19-20) ״אִם תֹּאבוּ״ etc. ״וְאִם תְּמָאֲנוּ״ etc. ״חֶרֶב״ etc. And it doubled the statement with different words — ״לִקְחִי אִמְרָתִי״ [my doctrine, my utterance] — corresponding to the two Torot: the Written Torah and the Oral Torah. Corresponding to the Written Torah it says ״לִקְחִי״, for HaShem gave it from His hand to the hand of Moses, written with the finger of Elohim; and although He gave only the Ten Commandments, within them is included everything written in the book of the Torah, as they said of blessed memory (Zohar vol. II 93:). And corresponding to the Oral Torah it says ״אִמְרָתִי״, for these are matters that were transmitted by spoken utterance, mouth to mouth. And it called the Written Torah מָטָר [rain] and the Oral Torah טַל [dew], because the Written Torah is the principal and foundation of everything, and the Oral Torah consists of repairs and precise rules that are not explicit in it but only alluded to. And this is the meaning the verse took in its comparison, that it likened the Written Torah to rain, which is the foundation and principal of all, and the Oral Torah to dew, which is the repair and maintenance of what the rain has produced — just as the Oral Torah is to the Written Torah." "<b>Further,</b> it intends according to what the men of truth said (Be'er Heiteiv, O.C. sec. 328, in the name of the Arizal) that the command of engaging in Written Torah is only by day and not by night. And so they said in the baraita (Midrash Shocher Tov 19:7) that Moses, during those forty days that he was with HaShem, could distinguish between day and night by the instruction that HaShem taught him: when He taught him Mishnah, he knew it was night. And there is a reason for the matter, that study of Scripture is not at night." "<b>And for this reason</b> it called the Written Torah מָטָר [rain], for it is not constant but only in the rainy season and not in the dry season. And go forth and learn what happened to Rava (Ta'anit 24b) because he troubled to bring down rains out of their season — so too Scripture is specifically in its designated time, by day and not by night. And it likened the Oral Torah to טַל [dew], just as dew is uninterrupted summer and winter, day and night, so too the Oral Torah — there is no time that is not its time. And do not find it difficult that the sun would cause the dew to evaporate, for the prevention is not from the side of the dew:" "<b>As the small rain, etc.</b> It will be explained according to what they said in the baraita (Midrash Mishlei 10): Rabbi Yishmael said — when they stand a person in judgment, if he has Scripture they ask him: why did you not study Mishnah? And if he has Mishnah they ask him: Talmud? And so on in this manner. Now, from the depth of the words of the baraita, the matter proves that one who has Scripture — they do not ask him about the Work of the Chariot, but rather one level according to his ability, beyond what he labored, which he was obligated to labor in a realm higher than that. And he says ״כִּשְׂעִירִם״ etc. ״וְכִרְבִיבִים״ etc., meaning: like the measure that HaShem drips small drops upon the grass, which is small, and כִּרְבִיבִים, which are large and thick drops, upon the vegetation that is large and can bear it — by this comparison are the commandments of the Torah, each one according to the strength that one can attain. And in this there is room to be lenient and to be stringent: lenient for one who cannot attain more, and stringent for one who can attain much, for they ask of him everything and even the Work of the Chariot."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:21:15.507636712Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}